---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { ladderById, stepHref } from "../lib/ladder";

/**
 * How to use the two practice pages, in the page's own HTML. It used to sit
 * under the apps themselves, where it was clutter; here it is a guide a
 * director can read, and search engines can too.
 */
const firstStep = Object.values(ladderById)[0];
---

<Layout
  title="How to Use abcSightReading | Sight-Reading for Choirs & Ensembles"
  description="How to use abcSightReading with your choir or ensemble: choral exercises in SATB, SSA and TTB at UIL levels 1 to 5, single-line sight-singing on solfège, and the abcStepByStep path from ta and ti-ti."
  themable
>
  <article class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-2 text-sr-ink-2 flex flex-col gap-4 pb-10">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold text-sr-ink">How to use abcSightReading</h1>
    <p class="text-lg">
      Two pages do the work. <strong>Choral</strong> writes exercises in parts. <strong>Unison</strong> writes one line
      to sight-sing. Both write a new exercise every time you press Generate.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-sr-ink mt-4">Choral sight-reading in parts</h2>
    <p>
      Pick a voicing: four-part mixed (SATB), three-part treble (SSA), tenor-bass (TTB), or the two-part voicings the
      lower UIL levels use. Then pick a UIL level, or set the key, meter, rhythms and chords yourself. Each part stays in
      its own range, the voice leading avoids parallel fifths and octaves, and eight bars come out as a real phrase
      with a cadence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Project it, print it, or play it back on piano or choir sound with a count-in and a moving cursor. Show
      movable-do or fixed-do solfège under each part, or Roman numerals above, on the exercise already on screen.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3"><a class="sr-btn" href="/choral-sightreading">Open Choral</a><a class="sr-btn-quiet" href="/choral-sightreading?tour=1">Take the tour</a></div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-sr-ink mt-4">Sight-singing one line</h2>
    <p>
      Choose the scale degrees your singers know (do-re-mi, then up to so, then the whole scale), the rhythms they
      read, and how far a line may leap. Every exercise is new and starts and ends on do, mi or so. Show movable-do solfège under the notes,
      and Kodály rhythm syllables: ta and ti-ti, or the words your group already uses.
    </p>
    <p>
      Unison also writes compound meter: 6/8, 9/8 and 12/8, felt in dotted-quarter beats - two, three or four to a
      bar. The tempo counts dotted quarters (♩. = 60), the click sounds once a beat, and the count-in counts beats,
      not eighths. The rhythms change with the meter: three eighths, quarter-eighth, the dotted quarter and dotted
      half, with rests and sixteenths when you want them. Counting reads 1 la li, Kodály ti ti ti, and your own
      syllables can have compound words of their own. Choral exercises stay in simple meter for now.
    </p>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3"><a class="sr-btn" href="/sightreading">Open Unison</a><a class="sr-btn-quiet" href="/sightreading?tour=1">Take the tour</a></div>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-semibold text-sr-ink mt-4">Not sure where to start?</h2>
    <p>
      <a class="underline text-sr-action-fg" href={stepHref(firstStep)}>abcStepByStep</a> goes from rhythm alone,
      through a single line, to two, three and four parts, one new thing at a time. Rhythm-only drills and a count-in
      make it work for a whole class or a student practicing at home.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every practice page has a short tour: the <strong>?</strong> at the end of its settings, or Take the tour in
      the menu. The piano page has one too: <a class="underline text-sr-action-fg" href="/piano-sightreading?tour=1">take the piano tour</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Preparing for contest? See <a class="underline text-sr-action-fg" href="/uil-sight-reading">UIL sight-reading practice</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Layout>
